.Dd November 26, 2026
.Dt FAND 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fand
.Nd daemon to control PWM (Pulse Width Modulation) cooling fans
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.\" Manual configuration
.Nm
.Op Fl D Ar duty
.Op Fl o Ar temperature
.Op Fl p Ar period
.Op Fl P Ar period
.Op Fl t Ar temperature
.Op Fl T Ar temperature
.Fl d Ar duty
.Ar tempsensor
.Ar pwmdevice
.\" Use a cooling profile
.Nm
.Fl c Ar cprofile
.Ar tempsensor
.Ar pwmdevice
.\" Display a cooling profile
.Nm
.Fl c Ar cprofile
.Fl l
.\" Display sensor and PWM device status
.Nm
.Fl s
.Ar tempsensor
.Ar pwmdevice
.\" Other
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon can be used to control the cooling fan connected to
.Ar pwmdevice
based on the temperature retrived from
.Ar tempsensor .
.Ar tempsensor
must be the name of the sysctl variable used to read the system temperature.
When
.Xr capsicum 4
is used, the sysctl variable must have the
.Pa CTLFLAG_CAPRD
flag.
.Ar pwmdevice
should be the name of the
.Xr pwmc 4
device that controls the target fan.
If an unqualified name is provided,
.Pa /dev/pwm
is automatically prepended.
There's two ways to configure the behavior of the
.Nm
daemon:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
manually using the
.Fl d ,
.Fl D ,
.Fl o ,
.Fl p ,
.Fl P
.Fl t ,
and
.Fl T
options
.It
or using a cooling profile with the
.Fl c
option.
.El
The cooling profile way is described in more details in
.Xr fand.cprofile 5 .
When configured manually, the low and high temperature thresholds are configured
using the
.Fl t
and
.Fl T
options, and their respective PWM parameters are configured using the
.Fl d ,
.Fl p ,
.Fl D
and
.Fl P
options.
The PWM can be disabled when temperature go under an off temperature threshold
using the
.Fl o
option.
When called with the
.Fl s
option,
.Nm
will print the current status of the temperature sensor and the PWM state and
exit.
This can be useful when determining the required temperature multiplier and
other options.
When called with both the
.Fl c
and
.Fl l
options,
.Nm
will print the corresponding cooling profile.
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-t temperature"
.It Fl c Ar cprofile
The path to the cooling profile to use.
Defaults to none.
Conflicts with the
.Fl d ,
.Fl D ,
.Fl t ,
.Fl T ,
.Fl o ,
.Fl p ,
.Fl P ,
.Fl t
and
.Fl T
options.
See
.Xr fand.cprofile 5
for details.
.It Fl d Ar duty
The duty cycle (in nanoseconds or percentage) of the PWM channel for the
default (low) temperature threshold.
Duty is the portion of the
.Ar period
during which the signal is asserted.
Conflicts with the
.Fl c
option.
.It Fl D Ar duty
The duty cycle (in nanoseconds or percentage) of the PWM channel for the
high temperature threshold.
Duty is the portion of the
.Ar period
during which the signal is asserted.
Conflicts with the
.Fl c
option.
.It Fl f
Run
.Nm
in the foreground.
Also enables more verbose output.
.It Fl i Ar seconds
The temperature polling interval, in seconds (Default: 60)
.It Fl I
Invert PWM signal polarity
.It Fl K
Converts the temperature sensor reading from Kelvin.
Conflicts with the
.Fl c
option.
.It Fl l
Show cooling profile, then exit.
Must be used in conjunction with the
.Fl c
option.
.It Fl m Ar multiplier
Multiplier applied to reading from temperature sensor.
Default to 1.0.
.It Fl o Ar temperature
Temperature to deactivate fan, in degrees Celsius.
Defaults to 50 °C.
Conflicts with the
.Fl c
option.
.It Fl p Ar period
The period in nanoseconds of the PWM channel for the default (low) temperature
threshold.
Defaults to 0.
Conflicts with the
.Fl c
option.
.It Fl P Ar period
The period in nanoseconds of the PWM channel for the high temperature threshold
Defaults to 0.
Conflicts with the
.Fl c
option.
.It Fl r Ar path
The PID file path.
Default to
.Pa /var/run/fand.pid .
.It Fl s
Show current temperature and PWM status, then exit.
.It Fl t Ar temperature
Default (low) temperature to enable the fan, in degrees Celsius.
Defaults to 50 °C.
Conflicts with the
.Fl c
option.
.It Fl T Ar temperature
High temperature threshold, in degrees Celsius.
Defaults to none.
Conflicts with the
.Fl c
option.
.El
When exiting or receiving a signal,
.Nm
will attempt to disable the PWM.
.Ss Signals
The following signals will trigger PWM deactivation on exit:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Dv SIGTERM
.It
.Dv SIGINT
.El
.Ss PWM Deactivation
Disabling the PWM may have a different effect than just enabling it
with duty set to zero, i.e., stopping the fan.
The behavior depends on the driver behind the
.Xr pwmc 4
controller.
For example, it can choose to enable an hardware cooling configuration instead
of just disabling the fan.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Using the "hw.temp.CPU" sensor and the pwmc1.0 controller, run the fan at half
duty cycle when the temperature reaches 45 °C and run at full when the
temperature rises above 60 °C.
Disable the fan if the temperature falls below 35 °C.
When reading from the sensor, assume value is in tenth Kelvin.
.Bd -literal
fand -t 45 -p 50000 -d "50%" -T 60 -o 35 -K -m 0.1 hw.temp.CPU pwmc1.0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pwmc 4 ,
.Xr fand.cprofile 5 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr pwm 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
